From eeea1fdb89ffbbe900617dab012204b30a2c8d53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 06:27:18 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Fit the stereo drawing area to the person's depth limits;
 Enable 3D

The drawing area is drawn for each eye whenever the desktop offers a
stereo visual: "Enable 3D" (stereo3d.enable in ng.opt, on by default,
saved at once when it is ticked) asks Togl for a quad-buffer widget at
the start, and a desktop without one gets the single picture, as
before, with nothing said.  The -stereo option still asks for it.

The separation was a constant 3 % of the distance looked at.  It is now
the largest under which every pixel of the drawing area stays inside
the person's depth limits, read through libstereo-declare on Togl's
window: before the two eyes the scene is drawn once from the camera
between them, its depth read back, and the nearest and farthest pixels
give the separation.  A change of the limits redraws the view (a
quarter-second poll).  Netgen has no separation setting.

The view options dialog gains "Enable 3D" and a button that opens the
desktop's 3D display settings.  When the limits are not offered, do not
arrive within two seconds, or cannot be read, the status bar says so
once; the sentences are in ten languages (Tcl's msgcat).

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic)
